                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 738.3915/86.</note>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Extract.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The American Minister to
                            Haiti to the Secretary of
                    State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Port au
                                Prince</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1911-11-23"><hi rendition="#italic">November 23,
                        1911</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 974.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Upon the receipt of the Department’s
                    November 22, 2 p.m., I called upon Secretary Legér and tried to urge a
                    conciliatory attitude pending the settlement of the boundary dispute, but he
                    absolutely refused to consent to what he styles an invasion. He says that if the
                    Dominicans do not withdraw the Haitian Government will exert every possible
                    means to force them to do so. He insists that the status quo line be respected
                    pending negotiations for the permanent settlement of the question.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">H. W. Furniss.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
